            <ArticleTitle>Current strategies for endometriosis management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>87-101</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ogc.2014.10.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8545(14)00098-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Endometriosis is a common gynecologic disorder that persists throughout the reproductive years. Although endometriosis is a surgical diagnosis, medical management with ovarian suppression remains the mainstay of long-term management with superimposed surgical intervention when needed. The goal of surgery should be excision or ablation of all visible disease to minimize risk of recurrence and need for repeat surgeries. When infertility is the presenting symptom, surgical therapy in addition to assisted reproductive technology can improve chances of conception; however, the treatment approach depends on stage of disease and other patient characteristics that affect fecundity.</AbstractText>
